[Unit]
Description=Hyperliquid fade LIVE bot (15m, avg-trade-size sizing) — REAL MONEY
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=simple
User=hyper
WorkingDirectory=/opt/hyperdata
EnvironmentFile=-/etc/hyperdata/telegram.env
# HL_ACCOUNT_ADDRESS + HL_SECRET_KEY (API wallet). Must be root-owned, chmod 600.
EnvironmentFile=/etc/hyperdata/live.env
# NOTE: --live is what arms real orders. Drop it for a dry run.
# Caps are deliberately small; raise them only after the live fill rate is measured.
# Total-count cap removed: 5 slots bound 47% of the time and only sustained ~31
# trades/day at the measured 3.8h hold against ~41 signals/day. max-positions is now
# 2x the per-side cap so it can never bind first.
#
# Directional exposure is bounded by --max-per-side instead, which is the risk that
# actually exists: signals arrive in correlated bursts. One market-wide move recently
# left EVERY position on EVERY arm long, and three same-direction entries once fired
# 38s apart. A total count does not bound that; a per-side count does.
#
# Gross 250 -> 2000. An artificial gross cap below the natural margin limit is the wrong
# constraint: $250 topped out near 8 positions and $700 near 22, both far below the ~67
# the collateral supports, so either would fire during exactly the clustered bursts that
# carry the edge (+56.5bps clustered vs -19bps isolated, see analysis/cluster_entries.py).
# $2,000 sits just under the natural ceiling ($712 spot x 3x = $2,138), leaving headroom
# for funding and fees, so the real backstops are now MARGIN and the per-side cap.
# Normal operation is nowhere near any of them: ~9 concurrent at ~$32 = 13% utilisation.
# The trade being accepted is that a full book puts most of the collateral at risk at once;
# isolated margin bounds the loss per position to its own margin.
#
# ATS sizing, base $24 (was flat $35). Reverted after 250 live flat-sized fills made the
# ats_ratio testable without sizing confounding it (analysis/ats_verdict.py, ats_tail.py).
#
# The mean effect is still not significant: ats beats flat by +$13.04 on live (t=+1.69)
# and +$23.34 on paper (t=+1.26), and those two arms share 94% of their signals, so it is
# one weak result rather than two. That is NOT why this is on.
#
# The reason is the tail, and both samples agree on it. The original thesis was that
# big-trade spikes fade harder, which predicts better WINNERS at high ats. Winners are
# identical -- +113.0 vs +126.9 live, +112.1 vs +122.1 paper, i.e. slightly worse. The
# entire effect is in the LOSERS: -194.2 vs -495.2 live, -164.9 vs -321.7 paper. Blowups
# past -400bps run 2 against 12 (live) and 4 against 14 (paper), and paper's blowup rate
# is monotone across ats quintiles at 8/7/6/3/1 percent. Per-trade sd falls from 405 to
# 178 bps between the halves. So a low ats_ratio -- a spike made of many small trades, a
# cascade with real participation -- is what blows up, and a high one is a single large
# print that reverts. Both liquidations were low-ats (SAGA 1.20, CASHCAT 1.54).
#
# This buys VARIANCE REDUCTION, not alpha. It is worth having anyway because the strategy
# has no stop-loss, so its risk IS the left tail, and a 6:1 blowup ratio needs far less
# data to establish than a 20bps mean difference ever will.
#
# $24, not $35: the ats multiplier averages 1.483, so $24 holds mean notional at $35.60 --
# today's exposure, unchanged. At $35 it would be $51.92, a 48% exposure increase riding
# along with the sizing change. The analysis normalised every rule to mean(mult)=1 exactly
# so "bet more" could not masquerade as "bet better"; deploying it any other way would
# reintroduce the confound it was built to remove. The 0.5x floor gives $12.00, clear of
# the $10 MIN_NOTIONAL; the 3.0x cap gives $72.00.
#
# --ab-rest-pct 0.5 runs a RANDOMISED experiment: half of all tight-spread signals are
# forced to rest instead of crossing. Wide-spread signals are untouched.
#
# Why it is needed: wide-spread entries earn +93.2bps against +3.6bps for tight ones
# (n=78 vs 152, t=+3.1), and that held on a clean 60-trade holdout after being wrongly
# rejected once (analysis/live_spread_holdout.py). But ALL 78 wide trades rested and 141
# of 152 tight trades crossed, so "wide spread" and "provides liquidity" are the same
# trades. Only randomisation separates them, and the answer decides whether the edge is
# about which COINS we pick or about HOW we enter -- the latter would apply to two
# thirds of all signals.
#
# The cost is bounded and small: tight-spread trades currently earn ~+3.6bps/trade, so
# if resting misses half of them the experiment costs roughly $1 a fortnight. Arms are
# assigned by hashing (symbol, 15m bar) rather than random(), so a restart cannot flip
# a signal's arm mid-decision. Read it with analysis/ab_cross.py, whose metric is P&L
# per SIGNAL, not per fill -- resting's whole cost is that it fills less often.
#
# --daily-loss-limit deliberately LEFT at 15. It is a loss brake, not a capacity cap,
# and its job does not change because more positions are allowed. It will now bind more
# often; raise it only if it is halting days that should have kept trading.
ExecStart=/opt/hyperdata/venv/bin/python /opt/hyperdata/live_bot_ats.py \
    --datadir /opt/hyperdata/live_15m_ats \
    --live \
    --notional 24 \
    --max-gross 2000 \
    --max-positions 40 \
    --max-per-side 20 \
    --daily-loss-limit 15 \
    --ab-rest-pct 0.5
Restart=always
RestartSec=15
StandardOutput=append:/opt/hyperdata/live_15m_ats/service.out
StandardError=append:/opt/hyperdata/live_15m_ats/service.err

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
